debris like pebbles and rock salt. Once we're done in the spray booth, the truck

242
00:15:55,701 --> 00:15:57,486
is now ready to be put back together.

243
00:15:57,487 --> 00:15:59,900
At this point, all body and mechanical repairs are

244
00:15:59,901 --> 00:16:05,120
done. As you can see, they're putting the final touches on this bad boy. He's water

245
00:16:05,121 --> 00:16:08,222
sanding and polishing to really give it that flat look.

246
00:16:08,223 --> 00:16:10,760
After he's done, it's basically out the door.

247
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:17,680
The Elizabeth Truck Center sometimes does more than just cure its smashed and dented patients.

248
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:20,800
It often sends them out the door with a facelift.

249
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:29,920
Truckers love shiny chrome and stainless, from exhausts to visors to boxes, but especially

250
00:16:29,921 --> 00:16:30,934
bumpers like this one.

251
00:16:30,935 --> 00:16:34,160
A bumper like this makes a statement when you're coming down the road.

252
00:16:34,161 --> 00:16:38,260
We have all the gauge covers and chrome extensions.

253
00:16:38,261 --> 00:16:45,020
You know, it's something that ain't a necessity, but I enjoy it, and it makes the truck stand out more.

254
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:52,520
While trailer lights, hood ornaments, custom steering wheels, grills, and exhaust pipes add some flash,

255
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:54,660
some truckers go all in.

256
00:16:55,140 --> 00:17:00,280
They spend tens of thousands of dollars transforming their rigs into rolling works of art.

257
00:17:00,281 --> 00:17:05,280
Take this tricked out Titan named the Beast.

258
00:17:05,281 --> 00:17:12,280
We fully customized the dashboard from the lighting to the sound system to the TV system,

259
00:17:12,281 --> 00:17:15,280
all the amplifiers which are all custom painted and matched.

260
00:17:15,281 --> 00:17:21,280
You head back here, all custom stainless from the deck plating, the trim,

261
00:17:21,281 --> 00:17:26,280
all the way back to the rear light panel which was all custom made by our fabricators.

262
00:17:26,281 --> 00:17:30,020
I'll tell you, I've been staring at it for about five years

263
00:17:30,021 --> 00:17:33,020
since we built it, and it still almost brings a tear to my eye.

264
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:40,120
And if you're one of the 209 million licensed drivers

265
00:17:40,121 --> 00:17:43,400
in the United States, one familiar variety of truck

266
00:17:43,401 --> 00:17:44,940
can bring a tear to your eye.

267
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:47,020
The tow truck.

268
00:17:49,580 --> 00:17:52,140
When its claws come out and your wheels go up,

269
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:54,260
it's either getting you out of trouble

270
00:17:54,261 --> 00:17:58,660
are making you suffer the consequences for getting into it.

271
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:03,100
You're a hero when you get there and their car's broke down.

272
00:18:03,180 --> 00:18:05,800
You pick them up and take them where they need to be safely.

273
00:18:06,280 --> 00:18:09,340
And then on the flip side of it, if they don't pay tickets

274
00:18:09,341 --> 00:18:12,100
and when the tow truck pulls up, you're the bad guy.

275
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:15,660
Hundreds of thousands of tow truck and recovery vehicles

276
00:18:15,661 --> 00:18:17,620
patrol America's streets and freeways.

277
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:20,380
It's a $5 billion a year industry.

278
00:18:20,381 --> 00:18:28,620
Every year, more than 3,000 tow trucks are produced here at the Jerdan Tow Truck

279
00:18:28,621 --> 00:18:31,550
Facility in Greencastle, Pennsylvania.

280
00:18:31,551 --> 00:18:35,560
Each is converted from an existing heavy-duty truck,

281
00:18:35,561 --> 00:18:39,700
and the backbone of every conversion is the subframe.

282
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:45,080
Every truck comes in, and the truck frame is a little bit different.

283
00:18:45,640 --> 00:18:50,080
So the subframe ties our piece of equipment into that truck frame.

284
00:18:50,380 --> 00:18:54,380
What we're seeing back here is the actual welding of that subframe.

285
00:18:54,381 --> 00:18:59,220
The robot will go through and check every touch point where it's going to weld.

286
00:18:59,221 --> 00:19:02,500
It will confirm that those tolerances are exactly right.

287
00:19:02,501 --> 00:19:06,020
Once the robot knows that those tolerances are exactly right, the robot commences to

288
00:19:06,021 --> 00:19:10,620
do a final finished welding on each of those weld points.

289
00:19:10,621 --> 00:19:15,820
The subframe functions as an adapter that connects a standard truck frame to the specialized

290
00:19:15,821 --> 00:19:20,040
towing equipment.

291
00:19:20,041 --> 00:19:23,040
The subframe supports a steel platform.

292
00:19:23,041 --> 00:19:26,920
Connected to the platform are the hydraulic system,

293
00:19:26,921 --> 00:19:30,000
the boom that holds the winch and its cables,

294
00:19:30,001 --> 00:19:34,080
and the undercarriage that lifts a vehicle.

295
00:19:34,081 --> 00:19:38,800
Finally, metal sheets form the body.

296
00:19:38,801 --> 00:19:43,040
This type of truck is called a self-loader.

297
00:19:43,041 --> 00:19:47,640
And before it tows, of course, it has to lift.

298
00:19:47,641 --> 00:19:50,520
And that takes leverage.

299
00:19:50,521 --> 00:19:53,820
It's kind of like a seesaw, that the rear axle

300
00:19:53,821 --> 00:19:55,820
being the fulcrum on the seesaw.

301
00:19:55,821 --> 00:19:58,020
So if we want to pick up something heavy,

302
00:19:58,021 --> 00:20:00,800
it's like having a fat guy on this end of the seesaw.

303
00:20:00,801 --> 00:20:04,320
The only thing we can do is put another fat guy, front axle

304
00:20:04,321 --> 00:20:06,460
weight, on the other end of the seesaw.

305
00:20:06,461 --> 00:20:08,700
We've got an adequate truck that

306
00:20:08,701 --> 00:20:10,400
has plenty of front axle weight.

307
00:20:10,401 --> 00:20:13,880
So therefore, we can maximize what we can pick up

308
00:20:13,881 --> 00:20:16,680
and keep the front end of the truck on the rear.

309
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:22,640
Redmond Towing in Lorton, Virginia typically picks up a couple thousand cars a year.

310
00:20:22,641 --> 00:20:29,640
And like many towing companies, it comes equipped with specialized trucks for every occasion.

311
00:20:29,641 --> 00:20:33,640
The industry's basic workhorse is the flatbed tow truck.

312
00:20:33,641 --> 00:20:39,640
Nothing fancy, a winch, a ramp, and a flat platform for the vehicle.

313
00:20:39,641 --> 00:20:44,640
It works fine as long as it's got plenty of maneuvering room.

314
00:20:44,641 --> 00:20:47,940
But what if the car to be towed is wedged in a virtually

315
00:20:47,941 --> 00:20:51,040
inaccessible parking space on the street?

316
00:20:51,041 --> 00:20:57,980
The answer is this sleek little model, the MPL-NG, the most

317
00:20:57,981 --> 00:21:02,100
nimble tow truck on the streets.

318
00:21:02,101 --> 00:21:05,900
In the hands of an expert driver, it can extract cars from

319
00:21:05,901 --> 00:21:11,840
the most congested spots with laser-like speed and precision.

320
00:21:11,841 --> 00:21:18,540
This truck has an underlift and it goes down.

321
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:21,080
The jaws go around the tires.

322
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:22,040
It's damage-free.

323
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:24,820
Just touches the tires, picks the car up.

324
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:32,260
A remote control lets the operator apply the long arm of the law from the safety and security of the driver's seat.

325
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:40,040
Any guesses how long it takes to snatch a car lodged between two others with just inches to spare on either end?

326
00:21:40,041 --> 00:21:43,660
Just watch the timer and don't leave the room.

327
00:21:53,380 --> 00:21:56,400
A 10-second reminder to pay your parking tickets.

328
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:04,120
When a towing job demands not nimbleness but brute strength, this monster answers the challenge.

329
00:22:05,700 --> 00:22:07,520
The Jerdan Rotator.

330
00:22:07,521 --> 00:22:13,640
It's the wrecker you see pulling semi-trailers out of the ditch on the shoulder of the highway.

331
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:20,720
Let's see how its operator goes about picking up this 50,000-pound disabled front loader.

332
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:26,000
First, the rotator takes its stance by stretching its legs.

333
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:34,160
There's four of these outriggers on the truck, and what they do is they stabilize the truck as I'm lifting,

334
00:22:34,300 --> 00:22:36,240
because this is a heavy load that we're picking up.

335
00:22:36,241 --> 00:22:41,000
You've got to put some crane pads down here to make sure we don't sink down through the

336
00:22:41,001 --> 00:22:42,360
asphalt.

337
00:22:42,361 --> 00:22:43,360
One for each one.

338
00:22:43,361 --> 00:22:50,040
Basically, you just slide it underneath and it'll come set down on it as we're preparing

339
00:22:50,041 --> 00:22:51,040
the lift.

340
00:22:51,041 --> 00:22:56,360
It creates a bigger surface area for the outrigger to set down on.

341
00:22:56,361 --> 00:23:01,540
So that brings the wheels up off the ground so the pressure is all on these.

342
00:23:01,541 --> 00:23:07,040
Now that we've got her up on platform, I can unlock the boom and start preparing for my

343
00:23:07,041 --> 00:23:08,040
lift.

344
00:23:08,041 --> 00:23:12,480
Once JJ moves his cables into position, he conducts the heavy lifting using only his

345
00:23:12,481 --> 00:23:13,480
fingertips.

346
00:23:13,481 --> 00:23:18,940
I'm going to lower it down a little bit more so I can set some of my rigging.

347
00:23:18,941 --> 00:23:24,260
I have a two-legged bridle, which basically turns one cable into two so I can get to both

348
00:23:24,261 --> 00:23:27,640
sides of the machine at the same time.

349
00:23:27,641 --> 00:23:28,341
J.J.

350
00:23:28,342 --> 00:23:36,480
centers the boom, the rotator lifts the tractor easily, then eases it on a flatbed

351
00:23:36,481 --> 00:23:41,600
for the trip to the repair shop.

352
00:23:41,601 --> 00:23:47,320
While tow truck operators perform a high-profile task we're all familiar with, other truckers

353
00:23:47,321 --> 00:23:52,040
serve society far from the limelight by getting down and dirty.

354
00:23:57,640 --> 00:24:04,420
The Secret Service orders about a thousand legally parked cars to be towed away every

355
00:24:04,421 --> 00:24:06,468
year in Washington, D.C.

356
00:24:06,469 --> 00:24:11,760
to provide maximum security for the president when he travels.

357
00:24:11,761 --> 00:24:14,820
American Trucking will return on Modern Marvels.

358
00:24:14,821 --> 00:24:18,960
We now return to American Trucking on Modern Marvels.

359
00:24:18,961 --> 00:24:28,960
Trucks, from long haulers to wreckers, handle the essentials of our daily lives.

360
00:24:28,961 --> 00:24:33,540
And if you've ever done some essential business inside one of the millions of porta potties

361
00:24:33,541 --> 00:24:39,100
that give us relief at public venues, you owe a debt of thanks to this truck and guys

362
00:24:39,101 --> 00:24:40,511
like Jeff Abbott of St.

363
00:24:40,512 --> 00:24:44,560
Louis, Missouri, one of the unsung heroes in the world of trucking

364
00:24:44,561 --> 00:24:47,360
that do society's dirty work.

365
00:24:47,361 --> 00:24:53,720
For six years, Jeff's been driving a vacuum truck designed for human waste removal.

366
00:24:53,721 --> 00:24:58,140
Think of it as a pooper scooper on wheels.

367
00:24:58,141 --> 00:25:03,300
Seven days a week, Jeff gets deep in you-know-what, cleaning our portable toilets with the help

368
00:25:03,301 --> 00:25:07,180
of his trusted truck and a good sense of humor.

369
00:25:07,181 --> 00:25:13,760
Oh, we hear the occasional jokes about just every toilet joke you could possibly think

370
00:25:13,761 --> 00:25:18,760
of or that job has to stink or just different things like that.

371
00:25:18,761 --> 00:25:22,760
People don't really like pulling up next to it or following behind it too much.

372
00:25:22,761 --> 00:25:28,760
It's not so much the truck that motorists fear so much as it is the tank and what's inside.

373
00:25:28,761 --> 00:25:36,760
But you can put those worries to rest thanks to waste tank manufacturers like Keevac in Kansas City, Missouri.

374
00:25:36,761 --> 00:25:42,760
Every tank they build here is airtight with one quarter inch thick walls.

375
00:25:42,761 --> 00:25:48,760
This is a 1,200-gallon aluminum portable toilet restroom tank.

376
00:25:48,761 --> 00:25:55,000
It's divided into two compartments, 900 gallons in the rear section for the waste and 300 gallons

377
00:25:55,001 --> 00:25:58,940
in the front section for the fresh water to fill the portable toilets.

378
00:25:58,941 --> 00:26:07,440
A full tank can weigh up to 26,000 pounds, a load that no one wants to see, and even

379
00:26:07,441 --> 00:26:08,940
a minor fender bender.

380
00:26:08,941 --> 00:26:13,620
Part of the scariest thing is that people don't know that you can handle the truck,

381
00:26:13,621 --> 00:26:19,240
and it almost, you have to drive defensively to protect yourself because these people think

382
00:26:19,241 --> 00:26:26,860
that you're coming at them or you don't know how to stop the truck behind them.

383
00:26:26,861 --> 00:26:31,520
The design of Jeff's truck helps ensure that the tank stays securely in place, even during

384
00:26:31,521 --> 00:26:33,360
a rough ride.

385
00:26:33,361 --> 00:26:40,480
The tank rests on tapered composite mounting boards that allows it to float atop the chassis,

386
00:26:40,481 --> 00:26:43,180
giving it some wiggle room.

387
00:26:43,181 --> 00:26:49,240
When the tank comes up and sits on this, there will be a gap here, and that gap is left intentionally.

388
00:26:49,241 --> 00:26:57,040
This little bit of a space allows the tank and the chassis to flex at different angles.

389
00:26:57,041 --> 00:27:01,740
After the tank is secured to the truck's chassis, assemblers follow a step-by-step

390
00:27:01,741 --> 00:27:05,740
process of installing the working components.

391
00:27:05,741 --> 00:27:10,580
The first is to put on the vacuum hose, and this will vacuum up the waste into the waste

392
00:27:10,581 --> 00:27:12,220
side of the tank.

393
00:27:12,221 --> 00:27:16,040
At the other end of the hose is a valve and wand assembly.

394
00:27:16,041 --> 00:27:20,240
This is what will go into the portable toilet to suck up the waste.

395
00:27:20,241 --> 00:27:23,220
This is the business end of the whole thing.

396
00:27:23,221 --> 00:27:27,500
The wand's sucking power comes from a pressurized pump.

397
00:27:27,501 --> 00:27:33,100
These trucks are capable of sucking 80 gallons a minute through a two-inch hose.

398
00:27:33,101 --> 00:27:37,900
I can demonstrate here with a five-gallon bucket of water.

399
00:27:37,901 --> 00:27:44,820
Now that's some sucking power.

400
00:27:44,821 --> 00:27:49,300
It's the real deal, of course, that awaits Jeff out in the field.

401
00:27:49,301 --> 00:27:53,000
And on a busy day, he'll empty as many as 80 portable toilets.

402
00:27:53,001 --> 00:27:57,520
When I first get out of the truck, I let the pump start up to create suction.

403
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:02,420
And then I go ahead and change all the toilet paper so that's done while that's building up.

404
00:28:02,480 --> 00:28:05,120
And then once that's done, I just start pumping them out.

405
00:28:08,340 --> 00:28:11,420
Most of the time the truck does all the work, we just got to guide it.

406
00:28:12,080 --> 00:28:14,940
The worst case scenario for truckers like Jeff is an overflow.

407
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:17,960
But the designers at Keevac have that covered.

408
00:28:17,961 --> 00:28:22,040
This is a stainless steel ball within a cage.

409
00:28:22,041 --> 00:28:27,060
As the liquid level rises inside the tank, the ball will float to the top and then get

410
00:28:27,061 --> 00:28:31,860
sucked up against the vacuum input here, and that will shut off the flow of the truck so

411
00:28:31,861 --> 00:28:35,960
that the truck can't overfill.

412
00:28:35,961 --> 00:28:38,960
What's really interesting, though, is you'll see the baffle in here.

413
00:28:38,961 --> 00:28:43,320
And the baffle has cutaways in it that allows some liquid to move forward and backward.

414
00:28:43,321 --> 00:28:48,960
And the baffle is what prevents all of the liquid from surging forward during braking.

415
00:28:48,961 --> 00:28:53,180
And if we didn't stop that surge, the truck would want to push through a stop sign or

416
00:28:53,181 --> 00:28:54,180
a red light.

417
00:28:54,181 --> 00:28:58,080
When all the sewage is out, a little fresh water goes in.

418
00:28:58,081 --> 00:29:02,080
And we take a five-gallon bucket and get the fresh water.

419
00:29:02,081 --> 00:29:04,200
We'll put that back in.

420
00:29:04,201 --> 00:29:08,280
Once that's done, we just wipe them down from top to bottom, make sure they're shining.

421
00:29:08,281 --> 00:29:12,380
If you thought this dirty job couldn't get any more unappealing, wait until you hear

422
00:29:12,381 --> 00:29:15,740
the kind of stuff Jeff typically finds inside the porta-potties tanks.

423
00:29:17,420 --> 00:29:19,467
You'll find all kinds of stuff.

424
00:29:19,468 --> 00:29:23,760
You'll find all kinds of spiders and bugs, and we found snakes in

425
00:29:23,761 --> 00:29:28,466
there. Dirty used underwear. My dad about 10 years ago found a gun in one.

426
00:29:28,467 --> 00:29:29,420
Had to call the

427
00:29:29,421 --> 00:29:31,569
police department to come get it out.

428
00:29:31,570 --> 00:29:34,880
But really, you name it, we've cell phones, a lot of cell

429
00:29:34,881 --> 00:29:35,581
phones.

430
00:29:35,582 --> 00:29:41,200
People will call from another cell phone and let us know that they, that the door swung and

431
00:29:41,201 --> 00:29:42,880
hit their cell phone and knocked it in there,

432
00:29:42,960 --> 00:29:45,300
and we'll have to tell them, did you put the lid down

433
00:29:45,301 --> 00:29:47,120
like you're supposed to at home, and they'll say no,

434
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:48,940
and then their cell phone will be in the bottom of it.

435
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:52,840
The port-a-potties offering the ultimate challenge for Jeff

436
00:29:52,841 --> 00:29:55,880
populate the venue with which we most associate them,

437
00:29:57,340 --> 00:29:59,100
the dreaded construction site.

438
00:30:00,480 --> 00:30:03,320
Jeff cleans as many as 40 toilets at one location

439
00:30:03,321 --> 00:30:06,780
and says the workers have a knack for complicating his job.

440
00:30:07,760 --> 00:30:11,100
When they run out of toilet paper, they go to other means.

441
00:30:11,200 --> 00:30:15,740
You know, paper towels, rags, painter's rags, clothes.

442
00:30:16,180 --> 00:30:19,800
It's a lot harder for these trucks to suck up a rag.

443
00:30:20,320 --> 00:30:22,420
When they get clogged, it's the worst part of the job.

444
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:26,300
And we have long kind of tweezer pliers on the truck,

445
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:29,240
so we have to take those and sometimes pull rocks out.

446
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:32,600
His daily rounds complete,

447
00:30:32,900 --> 00:30:35,000
and with a full tank sloshing behind him,

448
00:30:35,780 --> 00:30:39,180
Jeff makes one final stop to dump a load of his own

449
00:30:39,181 --> 00:30:41,980
at the local water treatment facility.

450
00:30:42,860 --> 00:30:45,360
First, we'll start by opening up our hose

451
00:30:45,361 --> 00:30:46,900
so we can create the gravity flow,

452
00:30:47,260 --> 00:30:48,780
natural flow into the manhole.

453
00:30:49,180 --> 00:30:50,780
Make sure we're latched in here.

454
00:30:51,660 --> 00:30:52,820
Don't want any problems.

455
00:30:54,600 --> 00:30:56,940
Takes about three minutes to dump a full truck.

456
00:30:58,000 --> 00:30:59,920
It may not be a job for everybody,

457
00:31:00,380 --> 00:31:01,860
but Jeff looks on the bright side.

458
00:31:02,700 --> 00:31:05,240
He credits the truck for doing the dirty work.

459
00:31:05,780 --> 00:31:06,960
He's just there to assist.

460
00:31:09,180 --> 00:31:10,860
Really, the truck does everything.

461
00:31:11,280 --> 00:31:14,560
This may not be the most glamorous job in the world, but it's a critical job.

462
00:31:14,900 --> 00:31:17,720
And with this big new truck, it makes my job that much easier.

463
00:31:19,860 --> 00:31:22,220
Another truck that's never been afraid to get dirty

464
00:31:22,221 --> 00:31:26,820
is the pride and joy of 87 million Americans.

465
00:31:28,340 --> 00:31:32,040
And one of the best-selling models has to run a treacherous gauntlet

466
00:31:32,041 --> 00:31:34,280
before it hits the showrooms.

467
00:31:34,281 --> 00:31:42,320
More than 180,000 garbage trucks do the dirty work of hauling away America's trash.

468
00:31:43,120 --> 00:31:50,440
The average truck travels 25,000 miles every year and gets less than 3 miles per gallon.

469
00:31:51,740 --> 00:31:54,720
American Trucking will return on Modern Marvels.

470
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:59,880
We now return to American Trucking on Modern Marvels.

471
00:32:04,280 --> 00:32:11,280
America's commercial truckers aren't the only ones who feel a deep bond with the vehicle they drive.

472
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:25,280
Truck enthusiasts among consumers feel an attachment just as profound with an iconic vehicle as American as hot dogs and apple pie.

473
00:32:25,281 --> 00:32:28,280
The Pickup Truck.

474
00:32:28,281 --> 00:32:32,280
About 3 million pickups are purchased every year in the United States.

475
00:32:32,281 --> 00:32:37,180
And the best-selling model over the past three decades has been the Ford F-150.

476
00:32:38,780 --> 00:32:45,700
Ford debuted its first mass-produced pickup truck in 1925, a Model T with a modified rear body.

477
00:32:46,960 --> 00:32:56,660
Ford unveiled its F-Series trucks in 1948, which have evolved over six decades into the burly yet stylish workhorses you now see everywhere.

478
00:32:56,661 --> 00:33:03,660
It's mass produced here at Ford's production plant in Dearborn, Michigan.

479
00:33:03,661 --> 00:33:09,660
If you'd been here about 100 years ago, you would have seen a small city comprised of

480
00:33:09,661 --> 00:33:17,660
about 100,000 Ford workers working on processes from steel to building engines to the stamping

481
00:33:17,661 --> 00:33:20,660
plant, cranking out units every day.

482
00:33:20,661 --> 00:33:29,600
Today, 3,500 workers command an army of robots, turning 3,000 individual parts into one F-150 pickup.

483
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:35,420
A finished truck comes off the line every 60 seconds.

484
00:33:38,120 --> 00:33:46,040
Cabs are assembled in one section of the plant, while in another, the rear ends, or boxes, gain their beds.

485
00:33:46,041 --> 00:33:53,200
Along the so-called trim line, workers groom the cab with seatbelts, carpet, and the instrument panel.

486
00:33:54,880 --> 00:34:05,449
The instrument panel consists of all your digital gauges, your speedometer, your RPMs, your radio, and all the instruments and all the electronics from the left side

487
00:34:05,450 --> 00:34:09,100
to the right side with all the heating and all the radio.

488
00:34:09,760 --> 00:34:12,960
It's like the number one thing that you see as soon as you get in the vehicle.

489
00:34:12,961 --> 00:34:16,840
We do approximately one of these every 50 seconds.

490
00:34:19,960 --> 00:34:24,160
The workers installing the wiring system operate at the same blistering pace.

491
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:31,540
Right now, he's installing the cooling system and the main wire harness for the unit right now.

492
00:34:31,700 --> 00:34:35,180
Each one's custom-made for the truck, that particular unit.

493
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:37,620
We can put the whole cooling system in less than a minute.

494
00:34:38,200 --> 00:34:41,220
Down the line, the cabs finally hook up with the boxes.

495
00:34:42,180 --> 00:34:44,680
Then that assembly is married to the frame and engine.

496
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:49,720
And there you have it, a brand new F-150.

497
00:34:51,400 --> 00:34:54,060
But before it reaches the showroom of your local dealer,

498
00:34:54,420 --> 00:34:56,240
it has to pull its weight here,

499
00:34:56,540 --> 00:34:59,000
at Ford's Michigan Proving Grounds.

500
00:35:01,240 --> 00:35:05,500
We're towing an 8,000-pound trailer up a 7% grade.

501
00:35:05,820 --> 00:35:08,880
As you're going up a grade, oftentimes you've got to come to a stop.

502
00:35:08,881 --> 00:35:16,080
You've got 8,000 pounds on the back there, so you don't want that load, the truck and the trailer rolling back on you.

503
00:35:16,081 --> 00:35:23,520
You come to a stop, take your foot off the brake, it holds, gives you time to get your foot to the accelerator, and proceed on up the hill.

504
00:35:23,521 --> 00:35:31,120
There's a sensor that knows that you're on an incline and engages the brakes and holds them, again, for that five seconds,

505
00:35:31,121 --> 00:35:34,880
to enable you to get your foot from the brake pedal to the accelerator.

506
00:35:34,881 --> 00:35:38,780
Test number two challenges the F-150's structural integrity

507
00:35:38,781 --> 00:35:41,520
on a devilish 300-foot obstacle course.

508
00:35:42,780 --> 00:35:45,500
It's difficult just holding 20 miles an hour,

509
00:35:45,620 --> 00:35:47,200
which is the speed limit on this course,

510
00:35:47,400 --> 00:35:49,980
with the bumps and the shakes

511
00:35:49,981 --> 00:35:52,420
and how much it throws you around the cab.

512
00:35:52,500 --> 00:35:54,340
But it's a good testament to the control

513
00:35:54,341 --> 00:35:56,340
that we have with the F-150

514
00:35:56,341 --> 00:35:58,240
and how it holds you in position.

515
00:35:58,400 --> 00:35:59,640
It allows you to control the truck

516
00:35:59,641 --> 00:36:01,760
under these incredibly rugged surfaces

517
00:36:01,761 --> 00:36:04,440
that you're probably not going to encounter

518
00:36:04,441 --> 00:36:08,420
in most everyday driving, but our heavy-duty users as well.

519
00:36:10,020 --> 00:36:12,960
The rough ride is a supreme test of the meeting point

520
00:36:12,961 --> 00:36:14,820
between the truck's cab and box.

521
00:36:15,980 --> 00:36:17,780
The frame is the foundation of the truck,

522
00:36:17,860 --> 00:36:19,900
and you've got to have a great foundation,

523
00:36:20,020 --> 00:36:22,220
a really stiff frame to be able to endure this.

524
00:36:22,460 --> 00:36:25,680
But you also have to have really secure attachments

525
00:36:25,681 --> 00:36:28,480
and mating points for the cab and the truck.

526
00:36:28,481 --> 00:36:31,560
And obviously, with the shape that we're experiencing here,

527
00:36:31,561 --> 00:36:36,480
It really tests out how well that cab is attached to the frame.

528
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:39,200
The meeting point holds.

529
00:36:40,200 --> 00:36:42,240
But what about the F-150's suspension?

530
00:36:43,900 --> 00:36:45,200
It's taking a real beating.

531
00:36:45,680 --> 00:36:48,640
And if you watch the back bumper, you can see it moving around there.

532
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:52,640
That suspension is really absorbing the shocks and the load of this really well,

533
00:36:52,700 --> 00:36:54,300
but it's staying right in the middle of the course.

534
00:36:54,520 --> 00:36:56,760
The frame is strong. The suspension is strong.

535
00:36:56,980 --> 00:36:59,800
You can see the truck stays perfectly even as it goes down the road.

536
00:36:59,801 --> 00:37:08,080
So we want those tires to move, and you can see them breathing as it comes in here, and the truck's absorbing all of that energy as it's coming up to a stop.

537
00:37:10,160 --> 00:37:18,120
This F-150 passed with flying colors and will soon join the 87 million pickup trucks now traversing America's roads and highways.

538
00:37:22,120 --> 00:37:28,120
And they'll be sharing those roads with the big rigs that, for many, define American trucking.

539
00:37:28,121 --> 00:37:32,120
You'll recognize them by the distinct Bulldog Hood ornament,

540
00:37:32,121 --> 00:37:35,120
a symbol with a surprising backstory.

541
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:48,120
The states with the highest number of pickup trucks per capita are North Dakota, South Dakota, and Montana.

542
00:37:48,121 --> 00:37:53,120
American Trucking will return on Modern Marvels.

543
00:37:53,121 --> 00:38:06,760
We now return to American Trucking on Modern Marvels.

544
00:38:06,761 --> 00:38:11,660
One American truck has won a reputation for toughness so enduring that its name is honored

545
00:38:11,661 --> 00:38:16,500
in a popular catch phrase.

546
00:38:16,501 --> 00:38:17,500
like a Mack truck.

547
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:27,380
To me, it's a truck driver's truck.

548
00:38:27,381 --> 00:38:29,320
It's a tough truck.

549
00:38:29,321 --> 00:38:33,080
It's a comfortable work truck, but they withstand

550
00:38:33,081 --> 00:38:34,460
a lot of punishment.

551
00:38:34,461 --> 00:38:36,980
They're good with the abuse, and they take it.

552
00:38:36,981 --> 00:38:38,720
I think a Mack truck, it's American.

553
00:38:38,721 --> 00:38:39,520
It's tough.

554
00:38:39,521 --> 00:38:42,320
It'll do anything, and it can get you there.

555
00:38:42,321 --> 00:38:46,500
I think it symbolizes the strength of American trucking.

556
00:38:46,501 --> 00:38:52,420
Mack trucks, named after the pioneering brothers who first produced them, have been making

557
00:38:52,421 --> 00:38:58,860
their indelible mark on the trucking industry for nearly a century.

558
00:38:58,861 --> 00:39:01,500
And this guy has quite a collection of them.

559
00:39:01,501 --> 00:39:08,420
And I got married in this truck.

560
00:39:08,421 --> 00:39:13,060
Gary Mahan of Basking Ridge, New Jersey, enjoys a committed relationship with these

561
00:39:13,061 --> 00:39:16,900
iconic vehicles, which date back as far as 1916.

562
00:39:19,460 --> 00:39:23,540
We believe this is the oldest unrestored Mack in the country. It's an AC Bulldog.

563
00:39:26,020 --> 00:39:30,900
Mack produced more than 40,000 Bulldogs between 1915 and 1939,

564
00:39:31,700 --> 00:39:34,500
which became the benchmark by which all other trucks were judged.

565
00:39:38,980 --> 00:39:41,940
The Bulldog earned its canine nickname during World War I.

566
00:39:43,060 --> 00:39:45,960
6,000 MAX served as cargo carriers,

567
00:39:46,400 --> 00:39:49,040
and British soldiers dubbed it the Bulldog

568
00:39:49,041 --> 00:39:51,060
because of its pug-nosed hood,

569
00:39:51,280 --> 00:39:54,260
as well as its dogged ability to get supplies to the front.

570
00:39:56,160 --> 00:39:59,200
But it was under the hood where the Bulldog got its bite.

571
00:40:00,500 --> 00:40:02,240
There's no radiator in the front.

572
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:04,060
It's been relocated.

573
00:40:04,180 --> 00:40:06,380
It was designed and built with the radiator to the rear,

574
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:08,620
and you've got a four-cylinder engine,

575
00:40:09,140 --> 00:40:12,580
60 horsepower initially, but had a lot of torque.

576
00:40:13,060 --> 00:40:18,000
After World War I, the Bulldog made its mark stateside,

577
00:40:18,680 --> 00:40:21,080
staking a claim as America's hardest-working truck.

578
00:40:22,440 --> 00:40:25,220
It hauled everything from gravel to gasoline,

579
00:40:26,420 --> 00:40:29,040
lighting the way through 30 years of American progress.

580
00:40:31,780 --> 00:40:34,340
Models of the 1920s lit up the night,

581
00:40:34,960 --> 00:40:37,260
not using electricity, but gas.

582
00:40:38,100 --> 00:40:40,400
For the headlights, turn your gas supply on,

583
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:41,640
acetylene in a tank.

584
00:40:41,641 --> 00:40:51,320
You come forward, open up your headlight bezel, strike it with a match, close your bezel, and away to go.

585
00:40:56,540 --> 00:41:02,040
Early models also lacked an electric starter, so they had to be hand-cranked.

586
00:41:04,140 --> 00:41:07,900
First thing we have to do is prime each of the four cylinders.

587
00:41:07,901 --> 00:41:10,720
So we give each one a little teaspoonful of gas.

588
00:41:11,640 --> 00:41:18,640
There it goes.

589
00:41:18,641 --> 00:41:25,140
Bulldogs of the 1920s could chug their way up the steepest grades with 10 tons on their

590
00:41:25,141 --> 00:41:32,140
backs, using a powertrain primitive by today's standards, a chain drive.

591
00:41:32,141 --> 00:41:33,140
Chain drive sounds spooky.

592
00:41:33,141 --> 00:41:38,140
I don't know what chain drive is.

593
00:41:38,141 --> 00:41:42,460
This is a chain drive feature found on the AC Mack Bulldogs.

594
00:41:42,461 --> 00:41:46,560
Had the little sprocket driving, a bigger sprocket to the rear.

595
00:41:46,561 --> 00:41:51,140
You had the ability to change your speed ratios by changing your sprocket sizes.

596
00:41:51,141 --> 00:41:56,780
If you're on a highway delivering goods, you'd want a faster speed, so you could increase

597
00:41:56,781 --> 00:41:59,200
the size of this small sprocket.

598
00:41:59,201 --> 00:42:05,260
But it was very dependable, and it forgave the shocks of the road conditions and soft

599
00:42:05,261 --> 00:42:08,260
so you wouldn't break an axle because the chain would take up

600
00:42:08,261 --> 00:42:11,260
the slack.

601
00:42:11,261 --> 00:42:15,260
Beginning in the 1930s, Mack began producing its own diesel

602
00:42:15,261 --> 00:42:18,260
engines, which made the Bulldog more reliable and cheaper to

603
00:42:18,261 --> 00:42:23,260
fuel up. As more competition entered the marketplace, Mack

604
00:42:23,261 --> 00:42:27,260
put the Bulldog down, shepherding in the next era of Mack

605
00:42:27,261 --> 00:42:29,260
trucks.

606
00:42:35,260 --> 00:42:37,760
At this point, Mack was heavy in the diesels.

607
00:42:38,180 --> 00:42:40,840
These are the vocational trucks that the customers wanted.

608
00:42:41,500 --> 00:42:46,400
At the end of the 1940s, Mack's chain drive was finally given the shaft,

609
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:49,960
as an improved technology became the industry standard.

610
00:42:51,980 --> 00:42:57,060
As you can see, the drive shaft brought the power from the transmission to a big center differential.

611
00:42:57,660 --> 00:43:02,380
In the differential, you had gears that sent the power out through the axles to your axle hubs

612
00:43:02,381 --> 00:43:04,560
on the outside of all of these big wheels.

613
00:43:04,561 --> 00:43:09,000
So it was a much better combination.

614
00:43:09,100 --> 00:43:11,960
Mack kept on trucking through the 20th century,

615
00:43:12,060 --> 00:43:14,300
further solidifying its bad boy status.

616
00:43:19,400 --> 00:43:23,080
We got into the R models, the 1985 vintage.

617
00:43:23,180 --> 00:43:25,680
This was Mack's most popular version.

618
00:43:25,780 --> 00:43:27,920
They sold over 400,000 units.

619
00:43:28,020 --> 00:43:31,480
Great truck, very vocational, did a lot of different things.

620
00:43:31,580 --> 00:43:33,660
And then they sold us the latest and greatest,

621
00:43:33,661 --> 00:43:37,360
which is a 2009 version, 21st century product.

622
00:43:41,100 --> 00:43:44,520
Today's rough-and-ready Macs are part of a brawny fleet of trucks,

623
00:43:44,760 --> 00:43:46,340
keeping America on the move.

624
00:43:48,740 --> 00:43:51,720
So wave a salute to the next trucker you see on the job.

625
00:43:53,860 --> 00:43:55,100
10-4, good buddy.

